#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} &= \\lim_{x\\to3} \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {3x^2}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3(3)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nso we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nBut that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ &=4. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty\n$$\n\nrespectively,\nso we have the case\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\n.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &= 0. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4}}$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\nso applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {1}{5} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b125944-1856-5ab1-beb7-434241d41c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264186} {:answer/id #uuid "b136a36e-4ad7-53f5-9af2-e8d0cdf01a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "2c451fa2-fc51-5189-9cb0-6f4c159f8621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264188} {:answer/id #uuid "619df33a-7843-5c74-9da5-34186caf63b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264189} {:answer/id #uuid "bc33fa1a-f686-5b11-9603-3f8b71680708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b136a36e-4ad7-53f5-9af2-e8d0cdf01a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad13d99-b787-5977-a513-f2b4171b3171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f71e509-23a7-5349-9c54-3e2d5f1db3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31257" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}+4x+3]^{′}}{[2x+3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295a7726-c40a-5634-b4bd-f9153064e185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264196} {:answer/id #uuid "070a9933-e030-5e90-91ee-ba93985c8446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264197} {:answer/id #uuid "96a4b77a-1bf6-5734-8a77-5f373a0b0900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264198} {:answer/id #uuid "4223010a-24af-52c6-8552-a359db7cafc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264199} {:answer/id #uuid "83eb24de-5359-5e54-a584-738f4806185e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4223010a-24af-52c6-8552-a359db7cafc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264199} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e33387-f9f3-5179-b023-40bc792c4b37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba7f82da-4dd1-526e-9dfc-73ae8a9ea727" :question/math-academy-id "q-31517" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04042993-3fef-5f55-95c5-115c760693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(9))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04042993-3fef-5f55-95c5-115c760693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(9))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46dd4f2-d324-5542-a7e1-3429ab75b92c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e0f7884-a830-5489-a8ed-a70ba5c64f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-336932" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8f872b-6dc6-53e9-9375-a11573728d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264252} {:answer/id #uuid "d5572dd1-40b5-5472-96e5-b8731a9f950c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264253} {:answer/id #uuid "9697133b-54a1-5709-8bdc-407fdbdd8b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264254} {:answer/id #uuid "032b7e57-209d-5e7f-97f2-53b407519105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} {:answer/id #uuid "99e51ad0-94fb-5a17-a2b1-81e5fa5b77bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b7e57-209d-5e7f-97f2-53b407519105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f36a378-4860-5a2e-8c9c-2f52d95b5aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21b4d0f5-e835-5e6f-8545-1cd83c1350d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72333" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{1/x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
